  "slug": "behavior-harmonic-68f4",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test closure validity, parameter-region description, and data fit of reduced reaction-network and gene regulatory models.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is formal analysis of dynamical models whose structure is specified first and then interrogated for stability, multistationarity, identifiability, or approximation validity. Two items address reaction-network theory: an ALT-graph and splitting algorithm for next-generation matrix stability analysis of biochemical reactions, and a Latin hypercube and adaptive sampling framework with surrogate models and CN2 rule induction that approximates parameter regions for multistationarity and multistability in mass-action systems. A moment-closure study assesses Gaussian, log-normal, and negative-binomial closures for bursty gene expression with nonlinear degradation, examining both stability and accuracy. Related modeling work examines whether simple compartmental models can reproduce multiple epidemic waves, and a reduced ODE model of aquatic decomposition tests identifiability with synthetic experiments. What is new is explicit assessment of approximation validity and parameter-region description, rather than model fitting alone.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-10-06"
}
